(i) the prior on \code{Q} (see \code{\link[=tvp_prior]{tvp_prior()}}) is informative and drives how much coefficient variation is found;
(ii) with many lags the state dimension (\eqn{K(Kp+1)}) is large relative to the sample, so
keep \code{p} small; (iii) the first \code{tau} observations are used up as a training sample and are not part
of the estimation sample; (iv) draws of \eqn{B_t} may be explosive at some dates: use
\code{reject_explosive = TRUE} for the (slow) Cogley-Sargent stability truncation.
